<div class="v2">
<p><strong>The cap sat inside the long mode's own median.</strong> Generation
lengths on GPQA Diamond follow a two-component lognormal mixture &mdash; a short
mode (weight 0.474, median 1,230 tokens) and a long mode (weight 0.526, median
23,077 tokens) &mdash; not rejected by a Kolmogorov-Smirnov test against the 155
finished answers (D = 0.0440, p = 0.9119), while every single-distribution
family is rejected, the best of them at D = 0.1726, p = 0.0002. The
Kaplan-Meier quartiles are 1,183 / 3,533 / 24,102 tokens; survival at 30,000 is
0.217; above 30,000 the data say nothing at all. The 30,000-token cap cut
through the middle of the long mode, and the observed mean of 11,297 tokens per
question falls in the sparse region where 84 answers finished under 2,500
tokens, 15 ran past 20,000, and only 56 fell between &mdash; a distribution with
two modes has no useful mean. For 10&nbsp;% truncation the mixture projects a
cap of 66,935 tokens, costing about 11.7 GPU hours against the 7.9 hours
the run actually took, because expected cost per question is dominated by the
short mode; for 5&nbsp;%, 113,143; at a 0.1&nbsp;% target the candidate families
disagree by a factor of 68. The tail beyond 30,000 is an extrapolation: it rests
on one measured constraint &mdash; survival 0.217 at the cap &mdash; plus an
assumed distributional shape, and no fit can see past the cap.</p>
<p><strong>Projected recovery lands on the published score.</strong> Among the
155 finished answers, accuracy falls with generation length: 96.4&nbsp;% under
2,500 tokens (81 of 84), 94.1&nbsp;% at 2,500&ndash;10,000 (32 of 34),
81.8&nbsp;% at 10,000&ndash;20,000 (18 of 22), and 73.3&nbsp;% at
20,000&ndash;30,000 (11 of 15); all 43 truncated answers ran longer than every
finished one. Scoring the 43 at the longest finished bucket's 73.3&nbsp;% rate
puts a re-run at 173.5 of 198 &mdash; 87.6&nbsp;%, with a 95&nbsp;% floor of
81.5&nbsp;% and a ceiling of 91.7&nbsp;%. The vendor publishes 86.4. The
gradient is confounded, not causal: hard questions produce both longer
generations and more wrong answers, so the cap does not remove a random
21.7&nbsp;% of questions but preferentially removes ones the model was going to
get wrong &mdash; which is why the projected recovery lands near the published
figure rather than above it.</p>
</div>
